1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,784
What should a service do when a setting is missing?

2
00:00:02,834 --> 00:00:07,032
A good cook won't start a dish with an ingredient missing, and won't guess a pinch.

3
00:00:07,082 --> 00:00:08,904
Our services are the same.

4
00:00:08,954 --> 00:00:10,968
No setting has a default value.

5
00:00:11,018 --> 00:00:18,480
A guessed timeout or a forgotten password that quietly falls back to a test value is how a system goes wrong in production.

6
00:00:18,530 --> 00:00:21,264
So every setting comes from one locked store, Vault.

7
00:00:21,314 --> 00:00:25,704
What only this environment knows, like its addresses, is typed in once.

8
00:00:25,754 --> 00:00:30,336
Every password and key is generated there, never typed into a file.

9
00:00:30,386 --> 00:00:34,728
Before a service starts, it is handed exactly its own settings.

10
00:00:34,778 --> 00:00:40,104
If even one is missing, it refuses to start, and names every missing one at once.

11
00:00:40,154 --> 00:00:43,968
That is fail fast: a wrong guess can never reach production.

12
00:00:44,018 --> 00:00:52,368
And rates, limits and waiting times are the bank's decisions, so each one is a setting the bank chooses, never a number hidden in the product.

13
00:00:52,418 --> 00:00:55,560
Next: how every call proves who it comes from.
